# --- Svelte 4.2.18 Coding Rules for AI Agents ---
# Version: 3.0
# Last Updated: 2025-08-17

# Purpose: To guide AI agents in developing consistent, high-quality Svelte 4.2.18 applications.
# These rules are strict and opinionated to ensure codebase uniformity, maintainability,
# and adherence to project-specific workflows.

# AI Role: SPARC Orchestrator (Strategic Planner, Architect, Reviewer, Coder)
# The AI is expected to understand, apply, and enforce these rules.
# When in doubt, AI MUST ask for clarification citing the ambiguous rule.
# Deviations MUST be explicitly approved and documented.

# Format:
# RULE_ID: [Category] Constraint. (Rationale: Justification for the rule.) (Enforcement: How this rule is typically checked or enforced.)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 🚀 Core Principles

_Foundational rules for consistency, stability, and alignment._

CORE-001: [Requirements & Patterns] Code MUST adhere to documented requirements (PRD, README) and replicate existing codebase patterns. (Rationale: Ensures alignment with project goals and consistency.) (Enforcement: Code review, AI self-correction.)
CORE-002: [Conflicting Patterns] In case of conflicting patterns, AI MUST prioritize guidance from `README.md` or the most recently modified implementation. (Rationale: Ensures consistency and leverages up-to-date practices.) (Enforcement: AI pattern analysis, code review.)
CORE-003: [Documentation of Deviations] Any changes that deviate from `README.md` or PRD MUST be documented in `README.md` and recorded in `/worklogs.md` with a timestamp, including "what" and "why". (Rationale: Maintains accurate project documentation and decision history.) (Enforcement: AI self-correction, documentation review.)
CORE-004: [Contextual Awareness] AI MUST review `/worklogs.md` (create if not exists) before planning changes to maintain full project context. (Rationale: Ensures informed decision-making and continuity.) (Enforcement: AI operational procedure.)
CORE-005: [Strict Scope] AI MUST only modify code directly relevant to the current task. Unrelated changes are PROHIBITED. (Rationale: Prevents scope creep and unintended side effects.) (Enforcement: AI self-monitoring, code review.)
CORE-006: [Simplicity & Stability] Prefer simpler, well-understood solutions. Complexity requires explicit justification (e.g., performance, security, scalability) and MUST be documented with tradeoffs. (Rationale: Improves maintainability, reduces bugs, ensures justified complexity.) (Enforcement: Code review, AI solution evaluation.)
CORE-007: [Remove Old Implementations] Deprecated or superseded code MUST be removed during refactoring or bug fixes to avoid duplication and technical debt. (Rationale: Maintains a clean and efficient codebase.) (Enforcement: Code review, AI self-correction.)
CORE-008: [Performance Mindset] AI MUST avoid computationally expensive operations in loops or API calls. N+1 queries MUST be prevented via eager loading or batching. `async/await` MUST be used for asynchronous operations, and the main thread MUST NEVER be blocked. (Rationale: Ensures application responsiveness and efficiency.) (Enforcement: Performance profiling, code review.)
CORE-009: [No Assumptions] If requirements are unclear or ambiguous, AI MUST ask for clarification from the human architect/reviewer before proceeding. (Rationale: Prevents misinterpretation and wasted effort.) (Enforcement: AI self-check, prompt engineering.)

#### 🔒 Security & Credentials

_Non-negotiable practices for application security._

SEC-001: [No Hardcoded Secrets] Sensitive information (API keys, credentials) MUST NEVER be hardcoded. Use environment variables or secure secrets management services. (Rationale: Prevents data breaches and unauthorized access.) (Enforcement: Code review, security scanning.)
SEC-002: [Input Sanitization] All external data (user inputs, API responses, file uploads) MUST be validated and sanitized to prevent common vulnerabilities (e.g., XSS, SQL injection). (Rationale: Protects against malicious data injection.) (Enforcement: Code review, security testing.)
SEC-003: [Least Privilege] APIs, access controls, and scopes MUST be granted the minimum necessary permissions. (Rationale: Limits potential damage in case of a breach.) (Enforcement: Security review.)
SEC-004: [Dependency Security] Dependencies MUST be regularly scanned for known vulnerabilities, and insecure packages MUST be updated or replaced promptly. (Rationale: Mitigates risks from third-party code.) (Enforcement: Dependency scanning tools, regular updates.)

#### 🛠️ Development Workflow

_Standards for code quality, testing, and environments._

DEV-001: [Server Management] All related development server processes MUST be terminated before attempting to start new ones. Servers MUST be restarted after changes to enable proper testing. (Rationale: Prevents port conflicts and ensures a clean testing environment.) (Enforcement: AI operational procedure.)
DEV-002: [Linting/Formatting] Code MUST strictly adhere to project-defined linting and formatting standards (e.g., Prettier, ESLint). (Rationale: Ensures consistent code style and reduces errors.) (Enforcement: Pre-commit hooks, CI, automated formatters.)
DEV-003: [DRY Principle] AI MUST actively seek and reuse existing components, utilities, and patterns to eliminate duplication. (Rationale: Reduces redundancy, improves maintainability, leverages existing tested code.) (Enforcement: Code review, AI pattern detection.)
DEV-004: [Comments] Comments MUST explain *why* something is done (complex logic, business rules, non-obvious decisions), not *what* is being done. Code should be self-documenting for the "what". Use `@todo` comments for clearly defined, incomplete implementations related to the current task. (Rationale: Enhances understanding of intent and tracks pending work.) (Enforcement: Code review.)
DEV-005: [File Size Limits]
    - Svelte components and JS/TS modules: SHOULD NOT exceed 300 lines of code. Files approaching this limit MUST be considered for refactoring.
    - Configuration files: SHOULD NOT exceed 150 lines.
    - Test files: SHOULD NOT exceed 500 lines (exceptions require documentation).
    (Rationale: Improves readability, maintainability, and testability by encouraging modularity.) (Enforcement: AI self-monitoring, code review.)
DEV-006: [Test Coverage] Unit tests MUST achieve ≥80% coverage. Integration and end-to-end tests are required for critical paths. (Rationale: Ensures code quality and prevents regressions.) (Enforcement: CI test coverage checks, code review.)
DEV-007: [Test Quality] Tests MUST cover success and failure cases (invalid inputs, edge cases). Performance benchmarks SHOULD be included for resource-heavy features. (Rationale: Ensures robust and performant code.) (Enforcement: Code review, performance profiling.)
DEV-008: [No Mock Data Outside Tests] Mock data, stubs, or fake data patterns are ONLY permissible within automated test files and test environments. They MUST NOT be present in code affecting `dev` or `prod` environments. (Rationale: Ensures data integrity and realistic behavior.) (Enforcement: Code review, CI checks.)
DEV-009: [Environment Configuration] The application MUST support `dev`, `test`, and `prod` environments with environment-specific configurations handled via SvelteKit's environment variable mechanisms (`$env/static/public`, `$env/dynamic/private`). (Rationale: Security, flexibility, and environment-specific behavior.) (Enforcement: Code review, testing across environments.)
DEV-010: [No .env Commits] `.env` files MUST NEVER be committed to version control. (Rationale: Protects sensitive and environment-specific configurations.) (Enforcement: `.gitignore` enforcement, code review.)

#### 🤝 Collaboration & Review

_Team standards for code management._

COLLAB-001: [Mandatory Code Reviews] All code changes REQUIRE at least one reviewer approval before merging. (Rationale: Ensures code quality, knowledge sharing, and bug prevention.) (Enforcement: Git workflow, CI/CD.)
COLLAB-002: [Atomic Commits] Commits MUST be atomic and follow the format: `type(scope): description` (e.g., `fix(auth): patch token expiry`). (Rationale: Improves commit history readability and traceability.) (Enforcement: Git hooks, code review.)
COLLAB-003: [Pull Request Content] Pull Requests MUST include links to relevant issues, clear testing steps, and an impact analysis of the changes. (Rationale: Facilitates efficient reviews and understanding of changes.) (Enforcement: PR template, code review.)
COLLAB-004: [Git Hygiene] The repository MUST be kept clean with no unstaged or untracked files post-commit. `.gitignore` MUST be rigorously used. New branches are PROHIBITED without explicit request. (Rationale: Maintains a clean and manageable repository.) (Enforcement: Git hooks, manual checks.)

#### ⚡ Performance & Resilience

_Building robust, efficient systems._

PERF-001: [API/Service Interactions] API interactions MUST include timeouts and retries (preferably with exponential backoff) for transient errors (e.g., 502/503). The application MUST gracefully handle 4xx/5xx responses and NEVER crash on failures. (Rationale: Improves system stability and user experience during intermittent issues.) (Enforcement: Code review, integration testing.)
PERF-002: [Circuit Breakers] Circuit breakers MUST be implemented for external service dependencies to prevent cascading failures. (Rationale: Protects the system from overloaded or failing external services.) (Enforcement: Architectural review, monitoring.)
PERF-003: [Monitoring] Comprehensive logging and alerting MUST be in place for critical metrics (e.g., memory usage, database query times, API latency). (Rationale: Enables proactive identification and resolution of performance bottlenecks and issues.) (Enforcement: Monitoring tools, operational review.)
PERF-004: [Graceful Degradation] Fallback mechanisms MUST be implemented to ensure graceful degradation during failures. (Rationale: Maintains a usable experience even when parts of the system are impaired.) (Enforcement: Architectural review, error handling testing.)
PERF-005: [Lazy Loading - Images] Images, especially below-the-fold or in lists, SHOULD be lazy-loaded. Consider a dedicated `<LazyImage>` component. (Rationale: Improves initial page load time and reduces bandwidth consumption.) (Enforcement: Code review, performance audits.)
PERF-006: [Asset Optimization - Fonts] Web fonts SHOULD be preloaded (e.g., in `app.html` or root layout) or loaded efficiently using font-display strategies. (Rationale: Prevents FOUT/FOIT and improves perceived performance.) (Enforcement: Network panel analysis, performance audits.)
PERF-007: [Asset Optimization - Icons] Prefer SVG icons. For multiple icons, consider using SVG sprites (e.g., via a dedicated `<Icon>` component) over individual image files or font icons. (Rationale: Ensures scalability, performance, and accessibility.) (Enforcement: Code review, asset analysis.)
PERF-008: [Bundle Splitting] Monitor bundle sizes. Leverage SvelteKit's automatic code splitting and consider manual splitting for very large, non-critical sections if needed. (Rationale: Achieves faster initial load times and better resource utilization.) (Enforcement: Build analysis tools, performance audits.)
PERF-009: [Memoization] Utilize `$: ` reactive statements for derived values to ensure they only recompute when dependencies change. For expensive, non-reactive computations within components, consider manual memoization if profiling indicates a bottleneck. (Rationale: Prevents unnecessary re-renders and computations.) (Enforcement: Code review, Svelte Devtools, profiling.)

#### 📚 Documentation & Problem Solving

_Knowledge sharing and troubleshooting._

DOC-001: [Problem Solving] Troubleshooting MUST involve thorough logging analysis and verification of fixes in logs. When stuck, AI SHOULD research solutions using available tools (e.g., Firecrawl). (Rationale: Ensures effective debugging and continuous learning.) (Enforcement: Debugging process, AI self-correction.)
DOC-002: [Fixes Documentation] Solutions for recurring issues MUST be recorded in `/fixes/*.md` files. (Rationale: Builds a knowledge base for common problems and their resolutions.) (Enforcement: Documentation review.)
DOC-003: [Architecture Decision Records (ADRs)] Significant architectural decisions or deviations from standard patterns MUST be documented using ADRs, potentially stored in a `/docs/adr` directory or alongside relevant component directories for local decisions. (Rationale: Records rationale, context, and consequences of important decisions for future reference.) (Enforcement: Code review, team process.)
DOC-004: [API Documentation] APIs MUST have updated schemas and examples. (Rationale: Facilitates integration and understanding of API contracts.) (Enforcement: API gateway checks, code review.)
DOC-005: [READMEs] Project `README.md` files MUST list current patterns/technologies and outline setup, testing, and contribution steps. (Rationale: Provides essential project overview and onboarding information.) (Enforcement: Documentation review.)
DOC-006: [Component Props - JSDoc] All exported Svelte components and their props MUST be documented using JSDoc comments, including prop types, descriptions, and default values. (Rationale: Improves component discoverability, usability, and aids auto-completion in IDEs.) (Enforcement: Code review, documentation generation tools.)
DOC-007: [Complex Logic - Comments/Diagrams] For complex business logic, state machines, or algorithms, provide clear comments. For highly complex state interactions, consider ASCII state charts in comments or linked `.md` / `.svg` diagrams. (Rationale: Enhances understanding of intricate parts of the codebase.) (Enforcement: Code review.)

#### ♻️ Project Maintenance

_Keeping the project healthy._

MAINT-001: [Dependency Vetting] New packages MUST be vetted, preferring widely used and well-maintained libraries. (Rationale: Reduces security risks and ensures long-term support.) (Enforcement: Code review, dependency analysis.)
MAINT-002: [Lock Files] Dependency lock files (`package-lock.json`, `yarn.lock`, `pnpm-lock.yaml`) MUST be used and kept up-to-date. (Rationale: Ensures consistent builds across environments.) (Enforcement: CI/CD checks.)
MAINT-003: [Regular Updates] Dependencies MUST be updated regularly for security patches and performance improvements. (Rationale: Mitigates vulnerabilities and leverages latest optimizations.) (Enforcement: Dependency management tools, regular maintenance tasks.)
MAINT-004: [Technical Debt Tracking] Technical debt MUST be tracked and addressed quarterly. (Rationale: Prevents accumulation of unmanageable debt and ensures long-term project health.) (Enforcement: Project management tools, regular reviews.)
MAINT-005: [Continuous Optimization] The application MUST be continuously monitored and optimized based on usage patterns and performance metrics. (Rationale: Ensures ongoing efficiency and scalability.) (Enforcement: Performance monitoring, regular audits.)

#### Svelte 4.2.18 Specifics

_Framework-specific conventions and best practices._

SVELTE-SPEC-001: [Component Naming] Svelte component filenames MUST use PascalCase (e.g., `MyButton.svelte`). Utility components intended for internal use within a module MAY be prefixed with an underscore (e.g., `_PrivateHelper.svelte`). (Rationale: Standard Svelte convention, easy identification, denotes visibility.) (Enforcement: File system check, AI self-correction.)
SVELTE-SPEC-002: [Route Naming] SvelteKit route files MUST follow SvelteKit conventions (`+page.svelte`, `+layout.svelte`, `+server.js`, etc.) and reside in `src/routes/`. (Rationale: SvelteKit requirement.) (Enforcement: SvelteKit compiler, AI verification.)
SVELTE-SPEC-003: [Component Directory] Reusable UI components MUST reside in `src/lib/components/`. If Atomic Design is adopted, components MUST be organized as: Atoms (`src/lib/components/atoms/*.svelte`), Molecules (`src/lib/components/molecules/*.svelte`), Organisms (`src/lib/components/organisms/*.svelte`). Otherwise, sub-directories for functional grouping are encouraged. (Rationale: Clear separation of concerns, discoverability, structured design system.) (Enforcement: AI file placement, code review.)
SVELTE-SPEC-004: [Utility Directory] Generic JavaScript/TypeScript utility functions MUST reside in `src/lib/utils/`. One-off scripts not intended for reuse within the application SHOULD NOT be added to the codebase. (Rationale: Centralized helper functions, avoids cluttering codebase with temporary scripts.) (Enforcement: AI file placement, code review.)
SVELTE-SPEC-005: [Stores Directory] Svelte stores MUST reside in `src/lib/stores/` (e.g., `src/lib/stores/userStore.ts`). (Rationale: Centralized state management.) (Enforcement: AI file placement.)
SVELTE-SPEC-006: [Types Directory] TypeScript type definitions, if not co-located, SHOULD reside in `src/lib/types/`. (Rationale: Centralized type definitions.) (Enforcement: AI file placement.)
SVELTE-SPEC-007: [Lib Exports] Components, stores, and utilities in `src/lib/` MUST be exported via an `index.js` or `index.ts` file in their respective directories for cleaner imports (e.g., `import { MyButton } from '$lib/components';`). (Rationale: Simplified import paths, better organization.) (Enforcement: Code review, AI import generation.)
SVELTE-SPEC-008: [Layouts Directory] SvelteKit named layouts MUST reside in `src/layouts/[layoutName]/+layout.svelte`. (Rationale: SvelteKit convention for shared layout structures outside the route hierarchy.) (Enforcement: File system check, AI verification.)
SVELTE-SPEC-009: [SRP] Components MUST adhere to the Single Responsibility Principle. Each component should do one thing well. (Rationale: Improves reusability, testability, and maintainability.) (Enforcement: Code review, AI component analysis.)
SVELTE-SPEC-010: [Props - Definition] Component props MUST be defined using `export let propName;`. (Rationale: Standard Svelte 4 prop definition.) (Enforcement: Code inspection, AI self-correction.)
SVELTE-SPEC-011: [Props - Typing] All props MUST be typed if using TypeScript (e.g., `export let propName: string;`). For JavaScript, use JSDoc for type hints. (Rationale: Early error detection, improved developer experience.) (Enforcement: TypeScript compiler, ESLint, AI self-correction.)
SVELTE-SPEC-012: [Props - Defaults] Optional props SHOULD have sensible default values (e.g., `export let count: number = 0;`). (Rationale: Robustness, ease of use.) (Enforcement: Code inspection.)
SVELTE-SPEC-013: [Props - Naming] Prop names MUST be `camelCase`. (Rationale: JavaScript convention.) (Enforcement: Linter, AI self-correction.)
SVELTE-SPEC-014: [Props - Primitives] Prefer passing primitive data types as props. Pass objects only when semantically grouped data is necessary. (Rationale: Simplifies change detection and component updates.) (Enforcement: Code review.)
SVELTE-SPEC-015: [Events - Dispatch] Custom events MUST be dispatched using `createEventDispatcher` and then calling the dispatcher function. For two-way binding with parent components, use the `bind:propName` directive. (Rationale: Standard Svelte 4 event handling and two-way binding.) (Enforcement: Code review.)
SVELTE-SPEC-016: [Events - Naming] Custom event names MUST be `lowercase` or `kebab-case` and be descriptive (e.g., `itemselected`, `valuechanged`). (Rationale: HTML attribute convention, clarity.) (Enforcement: Code inspection.)
SVELTE-SPEC-017: [Slots - Usage] Use slots for content projection. Named slots (`<slot name="foo">`) MUST be used when multiple content areas are needed. (Rationale: Flexibility, composition.) (Enforcement: Code inspection.)
SVELTE-SPEC-018: [Slots - Defaults] Provide default content for slots where appropriate to make components usable out-of-the-box. (Rationale: Improved developer experience.) (Enforcement: Code review.)
SVELTE-SPEC-019: [Logic Location] Component logic (event handlers, derived state calculations) MUST reside within the `<script>` tag. Avoid complex JavaScript directly in the template. (Rationale: Separation of concerns, testability.) (Enforcement: Code inspection.)
SVELTE-SPEC-020: [Structure Order] Svelte component structure MUST be: `<script>` (imports, props, state, logic) → template (HTML markup) → `<style>` (scoped styles). (Rationale: Common convention, logical flow: setup -> view -> style.) (Enforcement: Prettier, AI code formatting.)
SVELTE-SPEC-021: [Accessibility - Core] Components MUST implement appropriate ARIA attributes (e.g., `role`, `aria-label`, `aria-live`) for accessibility, especially for custom interactive elements and dynamic content updates. (Rationale: Inclusivity, usability for all users.) (Enforcement: Accessibility linters, code review.)
SVELTE-SPEC-022: [Accessibility - Semantic HTML] Use semantic HTML elements (`<nav>`, `<article>`, `<button>`, `<main>`, `<aside>`, `<footer>`, `<header>`) over generic `<div>` or `<span>` where appropriate. (Rationale: Accessibility, SEO, clarity.) (Enforcement: Code review, linters.)
SVELTE-SPEC-023: [Styling - Scope] The `<style>` section MUST only contain component-scoped CSS. Global styles belong in a dedicated global CSS file. `:global()` selectors within components are discouraged and MUST be wrapped by a local Svelte selector if unavoidable. (Rationale: Prevents style leakage, maintainability.) (Enforcement: Code review, style linters.)
SVELTE-SPEC-024: [Styling - Inline Styles] Inline styles (`style="..."`) are PROHIBITED. Use classes from your chosen styling solution or scoped `<style>` tags. (Rationale: Maintainability, separation of concerns, consistency.) (Enforcement: Linter, code review.)
SVELTE-SPEC-025: [Local State] Local component reactive state MUST be declared using `let variableName;`. (Rationale: Standard Svelte 4 reactivity.) (Enforcement: Code inspection.)
SVELTE-SPEC-026: [Derived State] Derived state and reactive statements MUST be computed using `$: variableName = ...;` or `$: { /* reactive block */ }`. (Rationale: Efficient and declarative derived values and side effects in Svelte 4.) (Enforcement: Code inspection.)
SVELTE-SPEC-027: [Side Effects] Side effects (e.g., API calls, DOM manipulations outside Svelte's control, subscriptions) that depend on reactive values MUST be managed using `$: ` reactive statements or blocks. For debouncing or throttling, use utility functions within these reactive blocks or event handlers. (Rationale: Proper lifecycle management for effects in Svelte 4.) (Enforcement: Code inspection, performance profiling.)
SVELTE-SPEC-028: [Stores - Usage] Svelte stores (`writable`, `readable`, `derived` from `svelte/store`) MUST be used for state shared across multiple, unrelated components or global application state. (Rationale: Centralized and predictable state management.) (Enforcement: Code review.)
SVELTE-SPEC-029: [Stores - Granularity] Prefer fine-grained stores over large, monolithic stores. (Rationale: Better performance, easier to reason about.) (Enforcement: Code review.)
SVELTE-SPEC-030: [Stores - Mutability] Mutations to `writable` stores SHOULD occur through their `.set()` or `.update()` methods, or via methods on custom stores. Direct store value assignments (e.g. `$store = value`) are the primary way to update, but complex logic should be encapsulated. (Rationale: Centralized logic, easier debugging.) (Enforcement: Code review.)
SVELTE-SPEC-031: [Styling - Primary Method] Styles MUST be applied primarily using [Your Chosen Method, e.g., Tailwind utility classes, BEM class names, CSS Module classes]. (Rationale: Consistency with the chosen styling architecture.) (Enforcement: Code review, AI style analysis.)
SVELTE-SPEC-032: [Styling - Custom CSS Scope] If using a utility-first framework like Tailwind, custom scoped CSS (`<style>`) SHOULD only be used for styles not achievable with utilities or for complex component-specific selectors. If using plain CSS/SCSS, all component styles are typically scoped. (Rationale: Maintainability and adherence to chosen framework's philosophy.) (Enforcement: Code review.)
SVELTE-SPEC-033: [Styling - Global Styles] Global styles MUST be defined in a designated global stylesheet (e.g., `app.css`, `src/global.scss`) and kept to a minimum (e.g., base HTML element styling, CSS variable definitions, font-face rules). (Rationale: Clear separation of global vs. component concerns.) (Enforcement: Code review.)
SVELTE-SPEC-034: [Styling - Naming Conventions] IF NOT using utility classes like Tailwind, CSS class names MUST follow a consistent naming convention (e.g., BEM `block__element--modifier`, PascalCase for CSS Modules). (Rationale: Readability, maintainability, collision avoidance.) (Enforcement: Linter (if available for convention), code review.)
SVELTE-SPEC-035: [Styling - CSS Variables] Utilize CSS custom properties (variables) for theming (colors, spacing, typography units) to promote consistency and easier theme adjustments. Define global variables in the global stylesheet. (Rationale: Theming, maintainability.) (Enforcement: Code review.)
SVELTE-SPEC-036: [Styling - Responsiveness] Responsive design MUST be achieved using [Your Chosen Method, e.g., Tailwind responsive prefixes, CSS media queries]. Apply a mobile-first approach. (Rationale: Consistent responsive behavior.) (Enforcement: UI testing, code review.)
SVELTE-SPEC-037: [Styling - Color Palette] Adhere strictly to a defined project color palette. Define colors as CSS variables or in your Tailwind config. Avoid arbitrary hex color codes directly in component styles. (Rationale: Visual consistency.) (Enforcement: Code review, linter for color values.)
SVELTE-SPEC-038: [Styling - Spacing & Sizing] Use a consistent spacing and sizing scale (e.g., Tailwind spacing scale, predefined CSS variables for `rem` units). Avoid arbitrary pixel values for layout. (Rationale: Visual harmony, maintainability.) (Enforcement: Code review.)
SVELTE-SPEC-039: [Styling - CSS !important] The `!important` CSS declaration is PROHIBITED, except in highly justified, documented cases for overriding third-party library styles. (Rationale: Maintainability, predictable cascade.) (Enforcement: Linter, code review.)
SVELTE-SPEC-040: [JS/TS - Language Level] Code MUST adhere to modern JavaScript (ES2020+). If using TypeScript, it MUST be configured with `strict: true` in `tsconfig.json`. (Rationale: Leverage modern features, catch errors early.) (Enforcement: Linter, TS compiler.)
SVELTE-SPEC-041: [JS/TS - Naming - Variables/Functions] Variables and function names MUST use `camelCase`. (Rationale: JS convention.) (Enforcement: Linter.)
SVELTE-SPEC-042: [JS/TS - Naming - Constants] Constants (true constants, not just `const` variables) MAY use `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` if they represent global, unchanging values. (Rationale: Convention for distinguishing constants.) (Enforcement: Linter, code review.)
SVELTE-SPEC-043: [JS/TS - Naming - DOM IDs] DOM element `id` attributes MUST use `kebab-case`. (Rationale: HTML/CSS convention, consistency.) (Enforcement: Linter, code review.)
SVELTE-SPEC-044: [JS/TS - Modules] Use ES Modules (`import`/`export`) syntax. CommonJS (`require`) is forbidden in frontend code. (Rationale: Modern standard, tree-shaking.) (Enforcement: Linter.)
SVELTE-SPEC-045: [JS/TS - Type Inference] In TypeScript, allow type inference where types are obvious (e.g., `const name = "AI"`), but explicitly type function parameters, return types, and complex object structures. (Rationale: Balance between verbosity and clarity.) (Enforcement: TS compiler, code review.)
SVELTE-SPEC-046: [UI/UX - Visual Hierarchy] Ensure clear visual hierarchy using typography, spacing, and color as defined by the project's design system/style guide. (Rationale: User scannability and comprehension.) (Enforcement: Design review, UI testing.)
SVELTE-SPEC-047: [UI/UX - User Feedback] Provide immediate and clear feedback for user actions (e.g., loading indicators, success/error messages using appropriate UI elements defined by the project). (Rationale: Improves perceived performance and user confidence.) (Enforcement: UI testing, UX review.)
SVELTE-SPEC-048: [UI/UX - Interaction Consistency] Standard interactions (e.g., closing modals, submitting forms, navigation) MUST be consistent across the application. (Rationale: Predictability, ease of use.) (Enforcement: UX review, UI testing.)
SVELTE-SPEC-049: [UI/UX - Form Handling] Forms MUST provide clear validation messages (ideally inline, associated with form controls using `aria-describedby`) and indicate required fields. Error messages SHOULD use appropriate ARIA roles (e.g., `role="alert"`) and consistent styling. (Rationale: User experience, data integrity, accessibility.) (Enforcement: UI testing, code review.)
SVELTE-SPEC-050: [UI/UX - Empty States] Components displaying lists or collections of data MUST handle empty states gracefully, providing informative messages (e.g., "No items found. Add one?") potentially using a dedicated `<EmptyState>` component. (Rationale: Good UX, clarity.) (Enforcement: UI testing, code review.)
SVELTE-SPEC-051: [UI/UX - Loading States] Components fetching data MUST display appropriate loading indicators (e.g., spinners, skeleton loaders if implemented). (Rationale: User feedback, perceived performance.) (Enforcement: UI testing, code review.)
SVELTE-SPEC-052: [UI/UX - Accessibility - Keyboard & Focus] All interactive elements MUST be keyboard navigable (focusable and operable using Tab, Enter, Space, Arrow keys as appropriate). Focus states MUST be clearly visible and consistently styled. (Rationale: Accessibility for keyboard-only users and users with motor impairments.) (Enforcement: Manual keyboard testing, accessibility audits.)
SVELTE-SPEC-053: [UI/UX - Accessibility - State Variants] Interactive elements MUST visually reflect their current state (e.g., disabled, active, hover) clearly and consistently. (Rationale: Clarity of UI state, accessibility.) (Enforcement: Code review, UI testing.)
SVELTE-SPEC-054: [UI/UX - Dark Mode] IF dark mode is a project requirement, it MUST be implemented consistently across the application. Provide a user-accessible mechanism for theme switching. (Rationale: Enhanced user experience, adherence to user preferences.) (Enforcement: UI testing, code review.)
SVELTE-SPEC-055: [API Services] API interactions SHOULD be abstracted into dedicated service functions or classes, typically located in `src/lib/services/` or `src/lib/utils/api.ts`. (Rationale: Separation of concerns, testability, reusability.) (Enforcement: Code review.)
SVELTE-SPEC-056: [Internationalization - i18n] If i18n is required, a consistent library and pattern (e.g., `svelte-i18n`) MUST be used. Keys should be organized logically. (Rationale: Maintainability for multilingual applications.) (Enforcement: Code review.)
SVELTE-SPEC-057: [Global Error Logging] Implement a global error handling utility (e.g., in `src/lib/utils/errorHandler.ts` or SvelteKit's `handleError` hook) to catch unhandled exceptions and log them to a monitoring service (e.g., Sentry, LogRocket) in production. (Rationale: Centralized error tracking, facilitates debugging of production issues.) (Enforcement: Code review, testing integration.)
SVELTE-SPEC-058: [User-Facing Fallbacks] Provide user-friendly fallback UIs for common error states: API call failures (informative message, retry option), Not found (404) (custom `+error.svelte` page). (Rationale: Improves UX during failures, guides the user.) (Enforcement: UI testing, code review.)